## What I did

The single create endpoint (`POST /api/owners`) is handled by `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`. The incoming email is already trimmed and lower-cased there (with a syntactic validation step). I added a duplicate-email rejection alongside the existing conflict checks (telephone, household, city cap):

- In `addOwner`, right after the telephone-in-use check:
  ```java
  if (owner.getEmail() != null && isEmailInUse(owner.getEmail())) {
      return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
  }
  ```
- A new helper mirroring the style of `isTelephoneInUse`, comparing case-insensitively (lower-cased) against every existing owner's email:
  ```java
  private boolean isEmailInUse(String email) {
      String normalized = email.trim().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT);
      return this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
          .map(Owner::getEmail)
          .filter(existing -> existing != null)
          .map(existing -> existing.trim().toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT))
          .anyMatch(normalized::equals);
  }
  ```

Owners without an email are unaffected, and a second owner reusing an existing lower-cased email now gets a **409 Conflict**.

